" />"],[1161,"<Card title=\"Extensibility\" href=\"/docs/concepts/extensibility\" />"],[1191,"<Card title=\"Extensibility\" href=\"/docs/concepts/extensibility\" />"],[1092,"<Card title=\"Inference clients\" href=\"/docs/concepts/plugins/inference\" />"],[1121,"<Card title=\"Inference clients\" href=\"/docs/concepts/plugins/inference\" />"],[1091,"<Card title=\"Log stores\" href=\"/docs/concepts/plugins/log-stores\" />"],[1120,"<Card title=\"Log stores\" href=\"/docs/concepts/plugins/log-stores\" />"],[1216,"> **Economics metrics come free, separately.** Per-task **time, tokens, and\n> cost** are *not* part of this metric registry. They are harbor-native: they\n> come from each rollout's usage (surfaced via `Trajectory.metadata['usage']`\n> and aggregated into eval metrics), so you get them automatically without\n> registering or listing anything. The metric registry here is only for reducing\n> *rewards* to a scalar."],[37,"> **read past hypotheses + conclusions → form a hypothesis → launch → score →\n> write a conclusion → repeat.**"],[1287,"> **Which one do I use?** Use a **verifier-fn** for sub-millisecond per-task\n> checks where each task carries its own `expected` (tool-call matching,\n> exact-match, boxed answers). Use a **Verifier class** when the reward logic is\n> config-level and shared across tasks, or needs richer params via `Config`."],[495,"> Note: validation of `params` against each `.Config` is **lazy** by default\n> (happens at instantiation in the runner). `evsys validate --deep` /\n> `validate_yaml(path, deep=True)` forces it up front so unknown entry-point\n> extensions don't fail prematurely."],[273,"> Package: `evsys_sdk` · Distribution: `evsys-sdk` · Version: `0.1.0` · Python ≥ 3.12 · License: MIT\n> CLI entry point: `evsys`"],[570,"> These describe the *verification plan*. The runtime `Verifier` **Protocol**\n> (in `protocols.py`) is what actually executes verification — deliberately\n> separate concepts."],[173,"⚙️ The SDK handles everything else"],[172,"🧑‍💻 Coding agents customise"],[2485,"1.0 if `expected` is a substring of model\\_output."],[2486,"1.0 if regex `expected` matches model\\_output."],[2482,"1.0 if the model's emitted tool call matches expected; else 0.0."],[3,"1\\. Every experiment is standardised and stored"],[49,"1\\. Hello world (no GPU, no network)"],[219,"1\\. Install"],[243,"1\\. Install"],[838,"1\\. The raw data — JSONL rows you point the config at"],[805,"1\\. The raw data — prompts you point the config at"],[770,"1\\. The raw data — tasks you point the config at"],[265,"10\\. The registry & writing extensions"],[267,"11\\. CLI reference (`evsys`)"],[268,"12\\. Eval harness"],[269,"13\\. Dashboard client"],[270,"14\\. Public API surface"],[271,"15\\. Relationship to the backend repo"],[220,"2\\. Describe the experiment"],[50,"2\\. Drive everything from YAML"],[806,"2\\. How the teacher works (no extra config)"],[4,"2\\. Launch an experiment"],[244,"2\\. Quickstart"],[839,"2\\. The transform — shape raw rows into a chat conversation"],[771,"2\\. The verifier IS the reward"],[245,"3\\. Core concepts"],[51,"3\\. Matrix campaigns"],[221,"3\\. Run the wiring test (mock)"],[772,"3\\. The built-in `rl` algorithm"],[807,"3\\. The built-in `sdft` algorithm"],[840,"3\\. The built-in `sft` algorithm"],[8,"3\\. Train the model inside your harness (the agent plugin)"],[52,"4\\. Adding your own algorithm"],[773,"4\\. Benchmarks & validation — score it *during* training"],[808,"4\\. Benchmarks & validation — score it *during* training"],[841,"4\\. Benchmarks & validation — score the model *during* training"],[246,"4\\. The YAML schema (config models)"],[222,"4\\. Train for real (local)"],[9,"4\\. Validation & test — scored in the loop"],[53,"5\\. Adding your own verifier (reward function)"],[774,"5\\. Local logging — where the metrics show up"],[809,"5\\. Local logging — where the metrics show up"],[842,"5\\. Local logging — where the metrics show up"],[254,"5\\. Matrix campaigns"],[10,"5\\. Metrics — measure whatever you care about"],[223,"5\\. The three training paradigms"],[224,"6\\. Go further"],[11,"6\\. Logging — local and dashboard"],[54,"6\\. Real Tinker SFT"],[775,"6\\. Run it"],[810,"6\\. Run it"],[843,"6\\. Run it"],[255,"6\\. The run lifecycle"],[256,"7\\. Extension points & protocols"],[12,"7\\. Write the conclusion, then go again"],[259,"8\\. Built-in extensions"],[13,"8\\. Driving it with Claude Code"],[260,"9\\. Data shapes (Harbor interchange types)"],[1016,"A **backend** is the seam between an algorithm and the machine that runs it. Its\none job is to materialize a model plus its training resources (a client, a\ntokenizer, a service handle) and pass them along so the algorithm stays\nhardware-agnostic. You'd write your own to put the SDK on any **Tinker-compatible**\ntraining server — anything that exposes the Tinker training-server API, such as\nFireworks, TML, or SkyRL — without touching algorithm code."],[1046,"A **callback** is an object that gets called at fixed moments — when the\nexperiment starts, before/after each run, after every training step, on each\neval, on checkpoint, at the end. Callbacks are how logging, dashboards,\nprogress printing, and early stopping plug in without touching the loop. You\nlist them on `ExperimentConfig.callbacks`; you write your own when you need a\nside effect (ship to S3, push to a service, stop early) the built-ins don't\ncover."],[18,"a **conclusion** — what happened, auto-synthesized against your\n`success_metric` when the run finishes."],[1100,"A **data store** is the SDK's abstract file layer: every time an algorithm reads\na dataset or writes rendered rows / predictions, it goes through a `DataStore`.\nThe default is your local filesystem, so you rarely think about it — you make\nyour own when data lives somewhere else (a database, object storage, an\nin-process cache for tests)."],[17,"a **hypothesis** — why you're running it, set in `metadata.hypothesis`;"],[1168,"A **log store** is the sink the training loop writes to as it runs: scalar\nmetrics per step, the run's hyperparameters, and pointers to artifacts\n(checkpoints, files). The default writes JSONL under the run directory. You make\nyour own when you want metrics to flow somewhere specific (TensorBoard, a\ndatabase, several places at once)."],[1198,"A **metric** reduces a benchmark's raw rewards to a single number. A benchmark\nscores each task by running its verifier on `num_samples` rollouts, producing a\nlist of per-sample rewards per task; a metric takes that whole `list[list[float]]`\nand returns one float (`pass_rate`, `pass@3`, …). You list metrics by name on a\nbenchmark and write your own when you need a reduction the built-ins don't cover."],[151,"A **test benchmark** is the *final goal*: scored once after training (no\n`run_every`), tagged `[test]`. Model selection must never key off it."],[1228,"A **transform** is a small, named function-object that rewrites the rows of a\ndataset. Raw data on disk (`{query, answer, ...}`) almost never matches the\nshape an algorithm wants (`{messages: [...]}`); transforms bridge that gap. You\nlist them on a dataset, they run **in order**, and each one takes the rows the\nprevious one emitted. Write your own when your raw data has a layout no built-in\nknows how to read."],[152,"A **validation benchmark** is scored *during* training to drive model\nselection — same harbor format, tagged `[val]` with a positive\n`run_every`:"],[1256,"A **verifier** turns a model's completion into a reward. The SDK has **two\ndistinct verifier mechanisms** that are easy to confuse, so read this first:"],[735,"A `<Kind>Spec` model shaped as `kind` + `params`, and a `list[<Kind>Spec]`\nfield on whatever config owns it."],[2509,"A `Backend` is constructed once per run (typically by the Algorithm\ncomposer, see :mod:`evsys_sdk.algorithms.sft`). It owns the\nlive training client; everything else in the package operates against\nthis Protocol."],[646,"A `Benchmark` is your held-out test. If you select the best arm using\nbenchmark scores, you've leaked the test set — use `Validation` for selection."],[1931,"A `Sweep` is the OOP way to describe what YAML's `matrix:` block describes:\none `base_run` template + per-axis lists of values, expanded by cartesian\nproduct into N concrete `RunConfig` rows."],[1288,"A `Verifier` **class** can be registered from an external package via the\nentry-point group `evsys_sdk.verifiers` in its `pyproject.toml`:"],[1017,"A backend is any class satisfying the `Backend` protocol in\n`src/evsys_sdk/protocols.py`. It declares one ClassVar and implements two methods:"],[1801,"A backend's job is to materialise a model + training resources and hand\nthem to whichever algorithm runs. The Algorithm protocol is intentionally\nNOT parameterised by Backend: instead, the registry routes (recipe.kind,\nbackend.kind) to a specific Algorithm implementation."],[1374,"A benchmark on disk is a directory:"],[2449,"A benchmark scores each task by running its verifier on `num_samples` rollouts,\nyielding a list of per-sample rewards per task. A **metric** reduces that\n`list[list[float]]` (one inner list per task, holding that task's sample\nrewards) to a single number. Metrics are referenced by **string name** on a\nbenchmark's `metrics:` list and registered with `@register_metric`; add your\nown the same way in a project."],[30,"A benchmark's `metrics` come from the **metric registry**: score rollouts however\n*you* define success — `pass@1`, `pass@3`, `pass^3`, or the economics that come\nfree from Harbor usage (**cost / time / tokens** per task)."],[364,"A campaign"],[727,"A class with two ClassVars"],[769,"A complete, run-it-yourself RL walkthrough — tasks with verifiers as the reward, the built-in rl algorithm, rollouts via Harbor, in-loop validation, and local logging."],[804,"A complete, run-it-yourself SDFT walkthrough — prompts with gold answers, a frozen teacher built from the same model, the built-in sdft algorithm, in-loop validation, and local logging."],[837,"A complete, run-it-yourself SFT walkthrough — JSONL data, a transform, the built-in sft algorithm, in-loop validation, and local logging, explained line by line."],[959,"A custom agent ships as an ordinary Python class — the RL config references it by\nits `module.path:ClassName` import string, so any installed package (or your own\nproject module) works with no SDK fork. The engine imports it by that path at\nrollout time, exactly as it imports the built-in `BasicLoopAgent`."],[957,"A custom harness is any harbor `BaseAgent` whose `run` drives your loop and\nwrites the rollout onto the `AgentContext`:"],[171,"A declarative framework for LLM training experiments — SFT, RL, and distillation from a single YAML, built so coding agents can launch experiments, learn from them, and train models continuously."],[274,"A declarative, modular framework for LLM training experiments (SFT, RL,\ndistillation, prompt tuning). A single YAML file describes a full experiment —\n**data → train → eval** — and every moving part (algorithm, backend, metric,\nstore, …) is a pluggable extension registered by a decorator. The same YAML\nruns on a mock backend (tests), locally on TRL/PEFT, or remotely on Tinker."],[729,"A factory"],[2656,"A failing callback never kills the loop — the exception is logged at\nWARNING and the loop continues. Same pattern as failing evaluators."],[1862,"A few protocols are runtime-checkable so the registry can give better error\nmessages, but most are duck-typed."],[2863,"a final \"final\" checkpoint at the end of training."],[819,"A frozen **teacher** — the same base model, but shown the gold `expected`\nanswer as an in-context demo — scores the rollout's **top-K** next-token\ndistribution. That's \"what a model that knew the answer would say.\""],[777,"A full walkthrough of on-policy RL for a beginner. Unlike SFT there's no \"right\nanswer\" to copy — the model **tries**, a **verifier scores the attempt**, and the\nreward pushes the policy toward higher-scoring answers. We'll teach a model to\nsolve simple arithmetic and put the answer in `<answer></answer>`."],[812,"A full walkthrough of self-distillation for a beginner. The trick: there's **no\nseparate, larger teacher model**. The same base model — shown the gold answer as\nan in-context hint — acts as the teacher, and the student is trained to match the\nteacher's *answer-aware* distribution while rolling out on its own. We'll teach a\nmodel to answer capital-city questions."],[205,"A guided tour of the five layers and the eight registries."],[1340,"A harbor-format eval suite loaded into memory."],[326,"A minimal config:"],[1184,"A multiplex config:"],[726,"A registry"],[1721,"A single training run (one cell in a campaign matrix)."],[38,"A sweep makes each step wider: declare a `matrix` and one YAML expands into many\narms (run concurrently), so the agent searches learning rates and methods in\nparallel and keeps the best:"],[671,"A test/validation firewall: `Benchmark` (once) vs `Validation` (in-loop)."],[1005,"A third-party package self-registers via a Python entry point in the\n`evsys_sdk.algorithms` group:"],[1034,"A third-party package self-registers via a Python entry point in the\n`evsys_sdk.backends` group:"],[1791,"A training recipe."],[1668,"A training-loop callback to attach, by registry name + params. e.g.\n`\\{kind: early_stopping, params: \\{metric: pass_rate, patience: 3\\}\\}`."],[851,"A transform is just a function with one contract: &#x2A;*`rows -> rows`**. You list\ntransforms in the config and they run in order. The built-in `jsonl_to_chat`\nrenders each raw dict into a chat turn using templates you provide:"],[874,"A typical `metrics.jsonl` looks like:"],[1261,"A verifier class declares two class vars and one method:"],[728,"A YAML surface"],[1807,"Abstract data sink+source."],[1445,"Accepts arbitrary named series via `**metrics` (e.g. `val_loss=…`,\n`kl_loss=…`) plus a `split` (`train`/`val`/`test`). The named\nkwargs (loss, accuracy, …) are folded into the same `metrics` map.\nNone values are dropped."],[1592,"Accepts three shapes:"],[2781,"Adapt our `HarborTask` rows (the `harbor_task` / RL + eval format) to\nharbor task dirs + native `TaskConfig`\\s — **scored** rollouts."],[2785,"Adapt raw prompts (the generation / \"prompt\" format) to harbor task dirs +\nnative `TaskConfig`\\s — **generation-only** rollouts (no verifier, no\nreward). Used by SDFT student rollouts."],[238,"Add an algorithm, verifier, or backend without forking."],[1916,"Adding a new algorithm/verifier/metric is one decorator + a Pydantic Config\nclass. No subclassing the library, no editing any list."],[1605,"After all benchmarks score, the flat back-compat fields\n(`arm.eval_metrics` / `eval_breakdowns` / `eval_seconds`)\nmirror the **primary** eval — the first `test`-tagged\npost-training row, else the first post-training row, else nothing."],[934,"Agents"],[2769,"Agents using this run in-process; there's nothing to upload/download, so the\nlog-sync steps harbor logs a warning for are harmless (the agent writes\ndirectly onto the `AgentContext`, which is harvested regardless)."],[1362,"Aggregate output of `Benchmark.score`."],[161,"aggregating `best_score` + `conclusion` and finalizing the experiment."],[741,"algorithm"],[85,"Algorithm"],[635,"Algorithm"],[1788,"Algorithm"],[2840,"Algorithm-precomputed per-step metrics (e.g. teacher entropy,\nreward stats). Merged into the per-step log row."],[1662,"Algorithm-specific parameters; validated against \\<Algorithm>.Config."],[1882,"algorithm.train(RunContext) -> RunResult."],[1658,"AlgorithmConfig"],[1323,"algorithms"],[669,"Algorithms"],[672,"Algorithms"],[963,"Algorithms"],[633,"Algorithms & Evaluation"],[1428,"Algorithms sometimes nest the manifest under a sub-directory\n(e.g. `\\<run_dir>/\\<sub>/checkpoints.jsonl`); search shallowest-first\nand return the first match. Returns `None` if no manifest exists."],[2424,"Alias for `mean_reward`."],[1207,"Alias of `mean_reward` (same `compute`, different name)."],[2232,"Alias-aware slug matching for tool-detection evals."],[2220,"AliasMatcher"],[1565,"All benchmarks scored against this arm — see `EvalResult`."],[2568,"All hooks are sync (`def`, not `async`). If you need to schedule\nasync work, spawn `asyncio.create_task(...)` from inside the hook."],[315,"All hosted inference clients"],[2988,"All rollouts sampled from one task (`num_samples` of them). The\ngroup-relative advantage baseline subtracts the within-group mean reward."],[528,"All self-register on `import evsys_sdk`."],[2229,"All slugs that count as a correct prediction for `expected`."],[2178,"All the\ncomposer plumbing lives in\n:class:`~evsys_sdk.algorithms.base.BaseAlgorithm`; SDFT supplies the two\nper-algorithm pieces:"],[2194,"All the composer\nplumbing (backend, step/save cadence, evaluators, loop wiring, artifacts)\nlives in :class:`~evsys_sdk.algorithms.base.BaseAlgorithm`. SFT only has to\nsay *how a batch is built*:"],[314,"all three frontier SDKs"],[1942,"Always called on a `RunConfig.model_dump()` result, so every node is a\ndict; intermediate keys missing on params-style dicts are auto-created\n(with `setdefault`), and a typo in a strict path is caught later by\n`RunConfig.model_validate`."],[1652,"Always-on local mirror of experiment data (wandb-offline style)."],[940,"An **agent** is the rollout harness for RL (and SDFT) — the harbor `BaseAgent`\nthat actually drives the model through a task, turn by turn, and records what\nhappened. It's pluggable on purpose: point at any harbor agent and the model\ntrains *inside* that harness, so it learns to use it. This is the same principle\nas Opus being trained inside the Claude Code harness — the policy adapts to the\nexact loop, tools, and message flow it will run under at inference time. You'd\nwrite your own to train against a multi-turn loop, a tool-use protocol, or a\ncustom environment."],[971,"An **algorithm** is a training recipe. It owns one thing the rest of the stack\ndoes not: *how each step's batch is built and which loss it trains under*.\nEverything else — backend allocation, step/save cadence, evaluators, logging,\nartifacts — is shared plumbing. **Any** algorithm is supported (LoRA on any\nmodel, any loss) by overriding a single method, so you write your own whenever\nthe recipe differs from SFT/RL/SDFT."],[1129,"An **inference client** is the thing that turns a prompt into generated text.\nEvaluators call it to produce predictions; RL rollouts call it to sample\ncompletions. The SDK ships clients for local HF models, Tinker, and the\nfrontier APIs (Claude, OpenAI, Gemini). You make your own to wrap any model or\nendpoint behind the same one-method contract."],[850,"An algorithm doesn't train on arbitrary dicts. SFT trains on a\n&#x2A;*`ChatMessagesRow`** — a conversation of `{role, content}` messages. A\n**transform** bridges the two."],[181,"An experiment is a single YAML file."],[2795,"An explicit `agent_import_path` wins (fully self-configured agent, no\nkwargs). Otherwise it's always :class:`BasicLoopAgent`, parameterized by\n`model_client`: `\"tinker\"` (on-policy `TinkerLLM`, needs `model_path`)\nor `\"litellm\"` (closed/API model; `model_name` is a litellm string, the\ntinker-only `model_path`/`renderer_name` are ignored)."],[1795,"And implement:"],[1537,"Anthropic-style base64 image block."],[1388,"API keys come from the standard provider env vars (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`,\n`OPENAI_API_KEY`, …)."],[1636,"API models a benchmark should also be scored on, from `models` (list)\nor `model` (single string) on the benchmark spec. Empty when neither —\ni.e. checkpoint-only, the existing behavior."],[2920,"Append completion tokens to a teacher prompt to form the sequence\nthe teacher will score."],[2692,"Append one row to the manifest on disk and remember it."],[431,"Applied in order to raw rows"],[1688,"Applied in order to the raw rows."],[138,"Apply `data.transforms[]` i